// ===================== LEARNING ENDINGS =====================
// All five use BG08 Review Room, soft piano, no alarms, nothing graphic. Each ends with Rewind
// to the start of the scene in its table, and a question for the player.

=== le1 ===
# ending: LE1 The Thin Roof
# rewind: 1.2
# scene: LE1 The Thin Roof
# bg: BG08
# hide: all
# hud: none
# prop: Figurine
# ambient: piano
# call: worried
NARRATOR: The storm passed through the thin roof like light through a curtain. Radiation can't be seen or felt while it happens. By evening the crew felt sick and tired, and help was days away. The crew did not make it home.
GROUND: Apollo 12 was saved by one switch someone remembered. Luck isn't a roof. # ref: ML1
MIMI: I think I'll build the thick roof myself.
LESSON: The Moon has no magnetic field and no air. Thick soil on the roof is the shield, and NASA sets a limit of 250 mSv for one storm. # ref: D2
QUESTION: If you could protect only one thing tonight, what would it be?
-> END

=== le2 ===
# ending: LE2 The Sleepy Air
# rewind: 2.3
# scene: LE2 The Sleepy Air
# bg: BG08
# hide: all
# hud: none
# prop: Small gauge panel
# ambient: piano
# call: curious
NARRATOR: Carbon dioxide has no smell and no colour. It started as headaches the crew blamed on a long day. By the time they were short of breath, their thinking was too slow to fix it. The crew did not make it home.
GROUND: Engineers call it normalization of deviance: a warning ignored once is easier to ignore twice. Skylab 4's crew spoke up about how tired they were. This crew didn't. # ref: ML3
MIMI: So you listen to the beeping even if you feel fine?
GROUND: Especially then.
LESSON: NASA's limit is 3 mmHg of CO2. Scrubbers run all day, because every person breathes out about 350 litres a day. # ref: D3
QUESTION: What small warning sign do you sometimes ignore?
-> END

=== le3 ===
# ending: LE3 Edge of the Shadow
# rewind: 3.2
# scene: LE3 Edge of the Shadow
# bg: BG08
# hide: all
# hud: none
# prop: Vial of ice
# ambient: piano
# call: curious
NARRATOR: The rover's battery had one job: carry the crew home. It spent that job on one more sample. On the crater floor, colder than minus 230 degrees, nothing makes heat. The crew did not come home. # ref: D5
GROUND: Apollo 11 landed with Mission Control counting the fuel out loud. Turn back at sixty. # ref: ML4
MIMI: So the rule isn't about being scared. It's about being able to come back.
GROUND: That's exactly right.
LESSON: Planners always keep a reserve. A trip that spends everything going out has nothing left for a surprise.
QUESTION: What do you plan to come back from, that you should pay for first?
-> END

=== le4 ===
# ending: LE4 Dust in the Dark
# rewind: 4.2
# scene: LE4 Dust in the Dark
# bg: BG08
# hide: all
# hud: none
# prop: Jar of gray dust
# ambient: piano
# call: curious
NARRATOR: The dust didn't attack anyone. It sat on the panels until the Power was gone. The crew kept the lab and the lights on, and turned the heaters down first. By the time the scrubbers slowed, the habitat was cold and everyone was tired. The crew did not make it home.
GROUND: When Mir was hit in 1997, its crew chose fast what stayed on. A list made before the emergency is a kind of rescue. # ref: ML8
MIMI: So the dust was small, but the problem was big.
GROUND: Most big problems start small.
LESSON: Essential systems (air, heat, shelter) come before useful ones (lab, rover) and nice ones (lights).
QUESTION: What three things would you never turn off?
-> END

=== le5 ===
# ending: LE5 The Last Checklist
# rewind: 5.3
# scene: LE5 The Last Checklist
# bg: BG08
# hide: all
# hud: none
# prop: Checklist card, one line circled
# ambient: piano
# call: worried
NARRATOR: There were eight steps. Seven were done perfectly. The crew was tired and ready to go home, and one step felt too small to matter. It was the one that mattered. The crew did not make it home.
GROUND: Apollo 13's explosion started with a small drop and one unchecked switch, years before launch. # ref: ML10
MIMI: Which step was it?
GROUND: That's the point. At the end, they all matter.
LESSON: Checklists catch the step that's easy to skip when everything else has gone right. The end of a mission is the worst time to hurry.
QUESTION: What step do you skip when you're almost done?
-> END
